('127.56.d.7.F',3,'Before buying frost equipment, a grower should compare:','["Brand slogans", "Colors", "Model names", "The cost with the value of the crop protected"]'::jsonb,3,'Economics guide the choice.','original'),
('127.56.d.7.F',4,'A frost system that costs more than the crop it saves is:','["Not cost effective", "Free", "Always worth it", "Required by law"]'::jsonb,0,'Costs must be balanced with benefits.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.A',3,'Pruning shears should be used:','["With dull blades", "With sharp blades and proper technique", "While running", "Without gloves"]'::jsonb,1,'Safe tool use prevents injuries.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.A',4,'A safe way to carry hand pruners in the field is:','["Held open while walking", "Open in a pocket", "In a holster or with the blade closed", "Thrown in a bucket"]'::jsonb,2,'Closed blades prevent cuts.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.B',3,'Dormant pruning is done:','["During harvest", "In summer only", "During bloom", "In winter when vines are not actively growing"]'::jsonb,3,'Pruning during dormancy minimizes stress.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.B',4,'Dormant pruning helps control crop load by:','["Limiting the number of buds left on the vine", "Adding buds", "Removing roots", "Watering more"]'::jsonb,0,'Fewer buds means fewer clusters.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.C',3,'Cane pruning leaves:','["Only short stubs", "Long canes with several buds each", "Only roots", "No wood"]'::jsonb,1,'Cane pruning retains fruiting canes.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.C',4,'Spur pruning leaves:','["Long canes only", "No buds", "Short two- or three-bud spurs on cordons", "Whole shoots"]'::jsonb,2,'Spurs are short fruiting units.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.D',3,'Precision viticulture uses technology to:','["Stop planting", "Ignore variation", "Replace all workers", "Manage vineyard blocks based on measured differences"]'::jsonb,3,'Data guides site-specific decisions.','original'),
('127.56.d.8.D',4,'A mechanical harvester helps vineyards by:','["Reducing the labor needed at harvest", "Planting rows", "Making grapes sweeter", "Raising labor needs"]'::jsonb,0,'Machines speed up picking.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.A',3,'Powdery mildew in vineyards is caused by a:','["Insect", "Fungus", "Bacterium", "Virus"]'::jsonb,1,'Powdery mildew is a fungal disease.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.A',4,'Grape berry moth larvae damage vineyards by:','["Sucking leaves only", "Chewing wires", "Feeding inside the fruit", "Eating roots"]'::jsonb,2,'Larvae burrow into berries.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.B',3,'Birds and deer are vineyard concerns because they:','["Pollinate vines", "Water vines", "Add nutrients", "Eat fruit and damage vines"]'::jsonb,3,'Animals reduce yields.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.B',4,'Netting in a vineyard is used to:','["Keep birds from eating the grapes", "Block light", "Add water", "Hold soil"]'::jsonb,0,'Nets are a physical barrier.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.C',3,'A component of IPM in a vineyard is:','["Ignoring thresholds", "Regular scouting for pests and disease", "Spraying weekly regardless", "Removing all insects"]'::jsonb,1,'Monitoring guides treatment.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.C',4,'Using beneficial insects to reduce pests is:','["Mechanical control", "Chemical control only", "Biological control", "Burning"]'::jsonb,2,'Natural enemies help control pests.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.D',3,'Opening the canopy by leaf removal helps reduce disease by:','["Adding shade", "Blocking sun", "Raising humidity", "Improving airflow and spray coverage"]'::jsonb,3,'Drier canopies get less fungus.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.D',4,'Removing prunings and mummified berries helps because they:','["Can harbor disease through winter", "Add nutrients", "Feed birds", "Cool the soil"]'::jsonb,0,'Sanitation reduces inoculum.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.E',3,'Before applying a vineyard pesticide, a worker should:','["Skip protection", "Read the label and wear required protection", "Guess the rate", "Mix products randomly"]'::jsonb,1,'The label is the law.','original'),
('127.56.d.9.E',4,'Following the pre-harvest interval on a label ensures:','["Poor flavor", "Lower yields", "Residues are safe when grapes are picked", "More chemical"]'::jsonb,2,'PHI sets a safe waiting time.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.A',3,'Soil texture describes the:','["Color of the soil", "Age of soil", "Smell of soil", "Mix of sand, silt, and clay"]'::jsonb,3,'Texture influences drainage.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.A',4,'Soil pH tells growers how:','["Acidic or alkaline the soil is", "Fertile it is only", "Wet it is", "Warm it is"]'::jsonb,0,'pH affects nutrient availability.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.B',3,'Which is a macronutrient for grapevines?','["Iron", "Nitrogen", "Boron", "Zinc"]'::jsonb,1,'Nitrogen is needed in large amounts.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.B',4,'Potassium deficiency in vines can show up as:','["Bright red roots", "Extra fruit", "Leaf edge browning", "Taller shoots"]'::jsonb,2,'K deficiency scorches leaf margins.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.C',3,'Clay soils hold nutrients better than sandy soils because they:','["Have less water", "Drain faster", "Are warmer", "Have more surface area for nutrient holding"]'::jsonb,3,'Clay particles hold nutrients.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.C',4,'Sandy soils often need more frequent fertilizer because they:','["Lose nutrients quickly through leaching", "Contain no air", "Are very acidic", "Hold all nutrients"]'::jsonb,0,'Water moves through quickly.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.D',3,'A vineyard needs 40 pounds of nitrogen per acre. Using 10 acres, how many pounds are needed?','["50 pounds", "400 pounds", "40 pounds", "4 pounds"]'::jsonb,1,'40 times 10 equals 400.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.D',4,'A fertilizer is 20 percent nitrogen. How many pounds of fertilizer supply 40 pounds of nitrogen?','["8 pounds", "800 pounds", "200 pounds", "60 pounds"]'::jsonb,2,'40 divided by 0.20 equals 200.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.E',3,'A good fertilization plan is based on:','["Neighbors'' habits", "Guessing", "Price only", "Soil tests and vine tissue analysis"]'::jsonb,3,'Tests show actual needs.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.E',4,'When presenting a fertilization plan, a student should include:','["Rates, timing, and expected costs", "Only the logo", "Nothing", "Weather forecasts only"]'::jsonb,0,'A plan covers what, when and how much.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.F',3,'Organic vineyards often build fertility with:','["Synthetic nitrogen", "Compost and cover crops", "Pesticides", "Herbicides only"]'::jsonb,1,'Organic systems rely on natural inputs.','original'),
('127.56.d.10.F',4,'Cover crops between rows help soil by:','["Packing soil", "Raising salt", "Adding organic matter and reducing erosion", "Removing nutrients"]'::jsonb,2,'Cover crops build soil health.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.A',3,'Grapevine water needs are highest:','["After harvest", "In winter", "During dormancy", "During rapid shoot and berry growth"]'::jsonb,3,'Active growth demands more water.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.A',4,'Mild water stress after veraison can:','["Improve fruit quality in some wine grapes", "Kill the vine", "Reduce sugar always", "Increase leaves"]'::jsonb,0,'Controlled stress concentrates flavors.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.B',3,'Choosing grape varieties that suit the local climate helps because they:','["Never need care", "Ripen well and resist local problems", "Ignore soil", "Grow without sun"]'::jsonb,1,'Matching variety to site improves success.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.B',4,'A late-ripening variety is risky in a cool region because it:','["Is sugar free", "Ripens too early", "May not ripen before frost", "Needs no heat"]'::jsonb,2,'Short seasons need early varieties.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.C',3,'Sandy soils need more frequent irrigation than clay soils because sand:','["Is richer", "Holds more water", "Is darker", "Holds less water"]'::jsonb,3,'Sand drains quickly.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.C',4,'High temperatures and wind increase the vineyard''s need for water by raising:','["Evapotranspiration", "Row spacing", "Soil clay", "Cloud cover"]'::jsonb,0,'ET is water lost by soil and plant.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.D',3,'Drip irrigation delivers water:','["Only by rain", "Directly to the root zone, slowly", "Over the canopy", "In a flood"]'::jsonb,1,'Drip is efficient.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.D',4,'A benefit of drip irrigation over overhead sprinklers is that it:','["Wets leaves", "Costs nothing", "Reduces leaf wetness and disease risk", "Uses more water"]'::jsonb,2,'Dry foliage reduces fungal problems.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.E',3,'Water for a vineyard may come from:','["Only fog", "Only snow", "Only dew", "Wells, rivers, or reservoirs"]'::jsonb,3,'Growers need dependable sources.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.E',4,'Water quality for irrigation matters because high salt can:','["Harm roots and reduce growth", "Boost growth", "Add flavor", "Help drainage"]'::jsonb,0,'Salts stress plants.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.F',3,'A tensiometer is used to measure:','["Air temperature", "Soil moisture tension", "Leaf color", "Fruit sugar"]'::jsonb,1,'It shows how hard roots must work for water.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.F',4,'A quick hand check of soil moisture uses:','["A ruler", "A thermometer", "The feel of a soil sample", "A scale"]'::jsonb,2,'The feel and ball test estimate moisture.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.G',3,'A block needs 1 inch of water per week. A drip system supplies 0.25 inch per irrigation. How many irrigations are needed?','["2", "8", "1", "4"]'::jsonb,3,'1 divided by 0.25 equals 4.','original'),
('127.56.d.11.G',4,'If rain supplies 0.5 inch of the 1.25 inches needed in a week, the irrigation must supply:','["0.75 inch", "1.75 inches", "1.25 inches", "0.5 inch"]'::jsonb,0,'1.25 minus 0.5 equals 0.75.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.A',3,'An advanced floral design student could aim for a path such as:','["Surgeon only", "Event floral director, studio owner, or buyer", "Welder only", "Judge only"]'::jsonb,1,'Advanced training opens higher roles.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.A',4,'Opening a custom wedding floral studio requires planning for:','["Only a van", "Nothing", "Startup costs, suppliers, and a client base", "Only a logo"]'::jsonb,2,'Entrepreneurs plan finances and customers.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.B',3,'An advanced designer shows professionalism during a busy wedding week by:','["Ignoring emails", "Missing deadlines", "Hiding delays", "Managing time and keeping clients informed"]'::jsonb,3,'Reliability under pressure matters.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.B',4,'A supplier sends the wrong stems the day before an event. A professional response is to:','["Tell the client, source replacements, and adjust the design", "Cancel quietly", "Use anything", "Hide the error"]'::jsonb,0,'Problem solving and honesty maintain trust.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.C',3,'Why should a designer keep floral knives and floral foam dust away from the face?','["To warm the shop", "To prevent injury and avoid breathing particles", "For better lighting", "To save time"]'::jsonb,1,'Safe habits prevent cuts and irritation.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.C',4,'A safe practice when lifting large containers of wet flowers is to:','["Drag them", "Throw them", "Use proper lifting technique or get help", "Lift with the back"]'::jsonb,2,'Safe lifting prevents injury.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.D',3,'A florist who signs a contract with fair terms is honoring:','["Hobby rules", "Social rules", "Casual agreements only", "Legal and ethical business responsibilities"]'::jsonb,3,'Clear contracts protect both sides.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.D',4,'Using a customer''s design idea without credit or permission can be:','["An ethical or legal problem", "Required", "Always allowed", "A compliment"]'::jsonb,0,'Intellectual property matters.','original'),
('127.57.d.1.E',3,'A designer shows citizenship by:','["Blocking sidewalks", "Providing flowers for community service events", "Refusing to participate", "Littering at markets"]'::jsonb,1,'Giving back builds community.','original')
