('127.57.d.8.E',4,'Ordering specialty flowers early is wise because they:','["Never run out", "May have limited availability", "Have unlimited supply", "Are always cheap"]'::jsonb,1,'Planning prevents shortages.','original'),
('127.57.d.8.F',3,'After completing an event, a designer should:','["Ignore the client", "Discard notes", "Evaluate results and gather feedback", "Skip review"]'::jsonb,2,'Evaluation drives improvement.','original'),
('127.57.d.8.F',4,'Comparing planned costs to actual costs after an event shows:','["Color mistakes", "Weather", "Nothing", "Where estimates were off"]'::jsonb,3,'Review improves future pricing.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.A',3,'A florist buys stems for $2 and uses a 3x markup. What is the retail price?','["$6", "$3", "$5", "$8"]'::jsonb,0,'2 times 3 equals 6.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.A',4,'A design costs $30 in materials and $20 in labor. With a 50 percent markup on total cost, the price is:','["$50", "$75", "$100", "$65"]'::jsonb,1,'Total cost 50, plus half of 50 equals 75.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.B',3,'A pricing policy is profitable if the price covers:','["Only materials", "Nothing", "All costs plus profit", "Only labor"]'::jsonb,2,'Price must exceed cost.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.B',4,'If discounts cause a design to sell below cost, the shop:','["Breaks even always", "Saves overhead", "Gains profit", "Loses money on that sale"]'::jsonb,3,'Selling below cost reduces profit.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.C',3,'A floral service contract should state:','["Services, price, dates, and payment terms", "Only a handshake", "Only colors", "Nothing"]'::jsonb,0,'Clear terms prevent disputes.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.C',4,'A deposit in a wedding contract is used to:','["Cancel the order", "Secure the date and cover initial costs", "Pay taxes only", "Replace payment"]'::jsonb,1,'Deposits reduce risk.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.D',3,'A floral budget should list the cost of:','["Only vases", "Nothing", "Each item, including labor and supplies", "Only flowers"]'::jsonb,2,'Per-item totals show true cost.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.D',4,'If a bouquet uses 12 stems at $1.50 each, the stem cost is:','["$12", "$24", "$15", "$18"]'::jsonb,3,'12 times 1.5 equals 18.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.E',3,'Good customer service in a flower shop includes:','["Listening and following through on commitments", "Interrupting", "Being late", "Ignoring requests"]'::jsonb,0,'Service builds loyalty.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.E',4,'When a customer is unhappy with a delivery, the florist should:','["Argue", "Apologize and offer a fair fix", "Ignore", "Blame them"]'::jsonb,1,'Handling complaints well keeps clients.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.F',3,'Building relationships with several growers helps a florist to:','["Avoid quality", "Rely on one source", "Have backup sources and better prices", "Lose options"]'::jsonb,2,'Variety of suppliers reduces risk.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.F',4,'A long-term vendor relationship can lead to:','["Delays", "Higher risk", "Fewer choices", "Reliable supply and possible discounts"]'::jsonb,3,'Trust benefits both sides.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.G',3,'The four Ps of marketing include product, price, place, and:','["Promotion", "Plants", "Pots", "Pruning"]'::jsonb,0,'Promotion is the fourth P.','original'),
('127.57.d.9.G',4,'Targeting brides through bridal shows is an example of:','["Random advertising", "Reaching a specific market", "Ignoring customers", "Reducing sales"]'::jsonb,1,'Target marketing focuses effort.','original'),
('127.57.d.10.A',3,'A professional organization in the floral industry is:','["A bank", "A car club", "The American Institute of Floral Designers", "A sports league"]'::jsonb,2,'AIFD is a design organization.','original'),
('127.57.d.10.A',4,'Industry organizations offer members:','["Free flowers", "Tax cuts", "Housing", "Education, networking, and certification"]'::jsonb,3,'They support professional growth.','original'),
('127.57.d.10.B',3,'A benefit of joining a floral association is:','["Networking with other professionals", "Fewer clients", "No benefit", "Isolation"]'::jsonb,0,'Contacts lead to opportunities.','original'),
('127.57.d.10.B',4,'Attending industry workshops helps designers to:','["Lose clients", "Learn new techniques and trends", "Forget skills", "Avoid learning"]'::jsonb,1,'Continuing education keeps skills current.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.A',2,'A plant and soil science student could consider a career as:','["Dancer only", "Referee only", "Agronomist, soil scientist, or crop consultant", "Poet only"]'::jsonb,2,'Plant and soil careers involve crops and land.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.A',3,'A student exploring the field should research:','["Weather", "Only salaries of unrelated jobs", "Fast food menus", "Education needs and job outlook for each role"]'::jsonb,3,'Information guides planning.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.B',2,'Which shows professionalism in a soil testing lab?','["Following procedures and labeling samples correctly", "Mixing samples", "Rushing and guessing", "Skipping labels"]'::jsonb,0,'Careful work builds trust.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.B',3,'A technician finds a possible sample mix-up. The professional response is to:','["Guess the result", "Report it and redo the test", "Delete the data", "Ignore it"]'::jsonb,1,'Honesty protects data quality.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.C',2,'Why wear goggles and gloves when handling soil chemicals?','["To keep soil clean", "To save time", "To protect eyes and skin", "To look serious"]'::jsonb,2,'Lab chemicals can injure.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.C',3,'A safe practice in a crop field is to:','["Skip water", "Wear no shoes", "Ignore heat", "Use sun protection and drink water"]'::jsonb,3,'Field safety prevents illness.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.D',3,'Rights and responsibilities on a farm job include:','["A safe workplace and following safety rules", "Skipping training", "Hiding injuries", "Ignoring rules"]'::jsonb,0,'Both employers and employees share duties.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.D',4,'Honest reporting of test data is an example of:','["Wasting time", "Ethical conduct in science and agriculture", "Cheating", "Ignoring rules"]'::jsonb,1,'Ethics demand accuracy.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.E',2,'Good citizenship in the plant and soil field includes:','["Ignoring erosion", "Dumping waste", "Protecting soil and water in the community", "Wasting water"]'::jsonb,2,'Stewardship benefits all.','original'),
('127.58.d.1.E',3,'A citizen-minded landowner helps prevent erosion by:','["Leaving bare ground", "Removing all plants", "Plowing slopes downhill", "Keeping ground covered with plants or mulch"]'::jsonb,3,'Cover protects soil.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.A',2,'A student notices a crop wilting in one part of the field and asks ''why?''. This is:','["Defining a problem from an observation", "Writing a recipe", "Ending an experiment", "Painting"]'::jsonb,0,'Questions start investigations.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.A',3,'A testable question in plant science is:','["Why do we eat?", "Does compost increase bean plant height?", "What is life?", "Are plants nice?"]'::jsonb,1,'Testable questions have measurable variables.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.B',3,'A fair experiment comparing two fertilizers on plants should:','["Skip measurements", "Change many things", "Change only the fertilizer and keep other conditions equal", "Use no plants"]'::jsonb,2,'Controls make comparisons valid.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.B',4,'Using several pots for each treatment helps to:','["Make it cheaper", "Hide data", "Eliminate controls", "Reduce the effect of chance"]'::jsonb,3,'Replication builds confidence.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.C',2,'Before a lab on soil chemistry, students should:','["Review safety rules and wear protective gear", "Skip safety", "Eat in lab", "Taste samples"]'::jsonb,0,'Safe habits prevent harm.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.C',3,'When diluting a strong acid for a soil test, add:','["Both at once", "Acid slowly to water", "Nothing", "Water to acid quickly"]'::jsonb,1,'Adding acid to water prevents splashing and heat spikes.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.D',2,'A microscope is used to:','["Measure soil temperature", "Weigh seeds", "Observe plant cells", "Water plants"]'::jsonb,2,'Microscopes magnify small structures.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.D',3,'A soil thermometer measures:','["Moisture", "Texture", "pH", "Soil temperature"]'::jsonb,3,'Temperature affects germination.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.E',2,'The SI unit for length is the:','["Meter", "Foot", "Pound", "Quart"]'::jsonb,0,'SI uses meters and centimeters.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.E',3,'Which is quantitative data from a plant experiment?','["It seems healthy", "The seedling grew 4.5 centimeters", "The leaves look nice", "The plant is pretty"]'::jsonb,1,'Quantitative data uses numbers.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.F',3,'A line graph of plant height over weeks shows:','["Plant names", "Soil color", "How growth changes over time", "The pot price"]'::jsonb,2,'Line graphs show trends.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.F',4,'A table of treatment averages helps to:','["Hide results", "Add confusion", "Remove data", "Compare groups at a glance"]'::jsonb,3,'Organized data supports analysis.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.G',3,'A diagram of the water cycle in a field is a(n):','["Model of a system", "Fertilizer", "Real rainfall", "Experiment"]'::jsonb,0,'Models represent processes.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.G',4,'A limit of a model of root growth is that it:','["Is perfectly accurate", "Simplifies the real, complex system", "Needs no testing", "Matches every soil"]'::jsonb,1,'Models leave out details.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.H',3,'A scientific hypothesis in plant science is:','["An opinion", "A proven fact", "A testable explanation or prediction", "A law"]'::jsonb,2,'Hypotheses are tested.','original'),
('127.58.d.2.H',4,'A scientific law, unlike a theory, usually:','["Cannot be tested", "Is a guess", "Explains why in full", "Describes what happens under certain conditions"]'::jsonb,3,'Laws describe patterns.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.A',3,'A greenhouse model is useful because it:','["Lets students test plant responses to controlled conditions", "Needs no data", "Replaces plants", "Is exactly like fields"]'::jsonb,0,'Models simplify variables.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.A',4,'A limitation of a small potted-plant model is:','["It uses no soil", "It may not match field conditions", "It cannot be repeated", "It is always perfect"]'::jsonb,1,'Scale limits generalization.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.B',3,'Looking at plant yield data, a student notices that most values cluster near 20 but one is 45. The 45 is a(n):','["Mean", "Median", "Outlier", "Mode"]'::jsonb,2,'Outliers differ greatly from others.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.B',4,'The mean of 10, 12, and 14 bushels is:','["36", "14", "10", "12"]'::jsonb,3,'Add and divide by three.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.C',3,'A field of 5 acres yields 40 bushels per acre. Total yield is:','["200 bushels", "8 bushels", "35 bushels", "45 bushels"]'::jsonb,0,'5 times 40 equals 200.','original'),
('127.58.d.3.C',4,'If soil sample A has pH 6 and B has pH 8, the difference is:','["48 units", "2 pH units", "1.3 units", "14 units"]'::jsonb,1,'8 minus 6 equals 2.','original'),
